><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250627111448+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250627111500+10'00'" name="c75f1760-71e6-45d8-a131-6069b0716a1a" page="5" coords="373.436000,235.320000,557.652000,235.320000,373.436000,225.525000,557.652000,225.525000,306.597000,224.888000,557.676000,224.888000,306.597000,215.093000,557.676000,215.093000,306.597000,214.400000,557.729000,214.400000,306.597000,204.605000,557.729000,204.605000,306.597000,203.969000,557.637000,203.969000,306.597000,194.174000,557.637000,194.174000,306.597000,193.481000,557.629000,193.481000,306.597000,183.686000,557.629000,183.686000,306.597000,183.050000,557.668000,183.050000,306.597000,173.254000,557.668000,173.254000,306.597000,172.562000,557.660000,172.562000,306.597000,162.766000,557.660000,162.766000,306.597000,162.130000,557.654000,162.130000,306.597000,152.335000,557.654000,152.335000,306.597000,151.642000,557.631000,151.642000,306.597000,141.847000,557.631000,141.847000,306.597000,141.211000,557.646000,141.211000,306.597000,131.416000,557.646000,131.416000,306.597000,130.723000,557.637000,130.723000,306.597000,120.928000,557.637000,120.928000,306.597000,120.292000,557.649000,120.292000,306.597000,110.496000,557.649000,110.496000" rect="306.291000,110.190000,558.035000,235.626000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study showed that perceived wellness was&#xD;associated with risk of health problems the following week in&#xD;young floorball players. A change towards higher stress, poorer&#xD;sleep quality and poorer well-being was associated with increased&#xD;risk of new injury, and higher stress and poorer well-being were&#xD;also associated with increased risk of illness. Players perceived&#xD;higher wellness and lower workload during school holidays, and&#xD;there should be an awareness that factors outside sport impact risk&#xD;of injury and illness. Athletes, parents, and coaches should pay&#xD;attention to early signals of impaired wellness, which may be used&#xD;to adjust activity e.g., training/match load. Information on wellness&#xD;factors could be included in injury prevention strategies for youth </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>athletes to facilitate safe sport participation.&#xD;Higher sport exposure was associated with decreased odds of&#xD;injury and illness in the subsequent week, and ACWR below 0.8 or&#xD;above 1.3 with increased risk of illness, which could be interpreted&#xD;as lower sport exposure possibly signalling upcoming health&#xD;problems. Well-designed training programmes with structured&#xD;progression for adequate training adaptation may be protective&#xD;against both injury and illness, though more research in recreational&#xD;youth players is needed.&#xD;In the present study we used floorball as a model to understand&#xD;stresses and strains of youth sport throughout a season. Although&#xD;caution should be exercised in applying the knowledge to other&#xD;sports, we believe that the findings are likely to be generalisable to&#xD;other similar team sports such as football, handball, and basketball.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="595.276000,143.320000,779.276000,235.320000"
/></highlight
